         <head>An Elegy on the Glory of Her Sex, Mrs. Mary Blaize</head>
         <author>Oliver Goldsmith</author>
         <p rend="color(#000000)">{{GUTENBERG_LICENSE}}</p>
         <p rend="start color(#000000)">en-gutenberg-odts/g/GoldsmithOliver-AnElegyOnTheGloryOfHerSexMrsMaryBlaize_2005.odt</p>
         <p>
            <lb/>Cover Text</p>
         <head>AN ELEGY</head>
         <head>ON THE GLORY OF HER SEX</head>
         <head>MRS. MARY BLAIZE</head>
         <subtitle>BY</subtitle>
         <pb/>
         <div type="div1">
            <head>An Elegy on the Glory of Her Sex, Mrs. Mary Blaize</head>
            <pb/>
            <div type="div2">
               <head>DR. OLIVER GOLDSMITH</head>
               <p rend="justify">watercolor painting</p>
               <table rend="frame" xml:id="Table1">
                  <row>
                     <cell>
                        <p rend="justify">Good people all,<lb/>with one accord,</p>
                        <p rend="justify">Lament for<lb/>Madam Blaize,</p>
                        <p rend="justify">Who never wanted<lb/>a good word—</p>
                     </cell>
                     <cell>line drawing</cell>
                  </row>
               </table>
               <table rend="frame" xml:id="Table2">
                  <row>
                     <cell>
                        <p>line drawing</p>
                        <p rend="justify">From those</p>
                     </cell>
                  </row>
               </table>
               <table rend="frame" xml:id="Table3">
                  <row>
                     <cell>
                        <p>line drawing</p>
                        <p rend="justify">who spoke her praise.</p>
                     </cell>
                  </row>
               </table>
               <p rend="justify">watercolor painting</p>
               <table rend="frame" xml:id="Table4">
                  <row>
                     <cell>
                        <p>line drawing</p>
                        <p rend="justify">The needy seldom pass’d her door,<lb/>And always found her kind;</p>
                        <p rend="justify">She freely lent to all the poor—</p>
                     </cell>
                  </row>
               </table>
               <table rend="frame" xml:id="Table5">
                  <row>
                     <cell>
                        <p>line drawing</p>
                        <p rend="justify">Who left</p>
                     </cell>
                  </row>
               </table>
               <table rend="frame" xml:id="Table6">
                  <row>
                     <cell>
                        <p>line drawing</p>
                        <p rend="justify">a pledge behind.</p>
                     </cell>
                  </row>
               </table>
               <p rend="justify">watercolor painting</p>
               <table rend="frame" xml:id="Table7">
                  <row>
                     <cell>
                        <p>line drawing</p>
                        <p rend="justify">She strove the neighbourhood to please<lb/>With manners wondrous winning;</p>
                     </cell>
                  </row>
               </table>
               <table rend="frame" xml:id="Table8">
                  <row>
                     <cell>
                        <p rend="justify">And never follow’d wicked ways—</p>
                        <p>line drawing</p>
                     </cell>
                  </row>
               </table>
               <table rend="frame" xml:id="Table9">
                  <row>
                     <cell>
                        <p>line drawing</p>
                        <p rend="justify">Unless when she was sinning.</p>
                     </cell>
                  </row>
               </table>
               <table rend="frame" xml:id="Table10">
                  <row>
                     <cell>
                        <p>line drawing</p>
                        <p rend="justify">At church, in silks and satins new,<lb/>With hoop of monstrous size,</p>
                        <p rend="justify">She never slumber’d in her pew—</p>
                     </cell>
                  </row>
               </table>
               <p rend="justify">watercolor painting</p>
               <table rend="frame" xml:id="Table11">
                  <row>
                     <cell>
                        <p>line drawing</p>
                        <p rend="justify">But when she shut her eyes.</p>
                     </cell>
                  </row>
               </table>
               <p rend="justify">line drawing</p>
               <table rend="frame" xml:id="Table12">
                  <row>
                     <cell>
                        <p>line drawing</p>
                        <p rend="justify">Her love was sought, I do aver,<lb/>By twenty beaux and more;</p>
                        <p rend="justify">The King himself has follow’d her—</p>
                     </cell>
                  </row>
               </table>
               <p rend="justify">watercolor painting</p>
               <table rend="frame" xml:id="Table13">
                  <row>
                     <cell>
                        <p>line drawing</p>
                        <p rend="justify">When she has walk’d before.</p>
                     </cell>
                  </row>
               </table>
               <p rend="justify">line drawing</p>
               <table rend="frame" xml:id="Table14">
                  <row>
                     <cell>
                        <p rend="justify">But now, her wealth and finery fled,<lb/>Her hangers-on cut short-all:</p>
                        <p rend="justify">The Doctors found, when she was dead</p>
                        <p rend="justify">Her last disorder mortal.</p>
                        <p>line drawing</p>
                        <p rend="justify">Let us lament, in sorrow sore,<lb/>For Kent Street well may say,</p>
                        <p rend="justify">That had she lived a twelvemonth more,—</p>
                        <p rend="justify">She had not died to-day.</p>
                     </cell>
                  </row>
               </table>
               <p rend="justify">watercolor painting</p>
               <p rend="justify">line drawing</p>
               <p rend="justify">back cover</p>
               <p rend="justify">Randolph Caldecott’s</p>
               <p rend="justify">Picture Books</p>
               <p rend="justify">
                  <hi rend="bold">"The humour of Randolph Caldecott’s drawings is simply irresistible, no</hi>
                  <lb/>
                  <hi rend="bold">healthy-minded man, woman, or child could look at them without laughing."</hi>
               </p>
               <p rend="justify">In square crown 4to, picture covers, with numerous coloured plates.</p>
               <lg>
                  <l>1 John Gilpin</l>
                  <l>2 The House that Jack Built</l>
                  <l>3 The Babes in the Wood</l>
                  <l>4 The Mad Dog</l>
                  <l>5 Three Jovial Huntsmen</l>
                  <l>6 Sing a Song for Sixpence</l>
                  <l>7 The Queen of Hearts</l>
                  <l>8 The Farmer’s Boy</l>
               </lg>
               <lg>
                  <l>  9 The Milkmaid</l>
                  <l>10 Hey-Diddle-Diddle and Baby Bunting</l>
                  <l>11 A Frog He Would a-Wooing Go</l>
                  <l>12 The Fox Jumps over the Parson’s Gate</l>
                  <l>13 Come Lasses and Lads</l>
                  <l>14 Ride a Cock Horse to Banbury Cross, &amp;c.</l>
                  <l>15 Mrs. Mary Blaize</l>
                  <l>16 The Great Panjandrum Himself</l>
               </lg>
               <lg>
                  <l>The above selections are also issued in Four Volumes, square crown 4to, attractive binding,</l>
                  <l>red edges. Each containing four different books, with their Coloured Pictures and</l>
                  <l>innumerable Outline Sketches.</l>
               </lg>
               <p rend="justify">1 R. Caldecott’s Picture Book No. 1<lb/>2 R. Caldecott’s Picture Book No. 2</p>
               <p rend="justify">3 Hey-Diddle-Diddle-Picture Book<lb/>4 The Panjandrum Picture Book</p>
               <lg>
                  <l>And also</l>
                  <l>In Two Volumes, handsomely bound in cloth gilt, each containing eight different books,</l>
                  <l>with their Coloured Pictures and numerous Outline Sketches.</l>
               </lg>
